Binh To and Jack Toyama

TinyFS Implementation

How well it works:
My TinyFS implementation works as expected and is able to correctly create, mount, write, read, rename, delete, and list files. 
The filesystem stores data in fixed-size blocks and uses metadata blocks to track file information such as file name, size,
timestamps, and block pointers. The test file we have shows that the implementation works as expected.  It preserves file data, updates metadata, and 
display useful file information. We kept the filesystem design simple using linked blocks rather than a more advanced indexing structure to store file data. 
This makes the implementation easier to understand and debug. Overall, the program works as intended, as demo'ed by our tinyFSDemo program.

One thing to note is that we have kept the number of customized error flags to a minimum amount to avoid confusion due to having too many error codes.

Additional Functionality

B. Directory Listing and File Renaming
    Tests additional function tfs_readdir() which prints name and size of every
    inode. tfs_readdir() also includes read_only flag from extra credit. Then 
    tests tfs_reaname(), which renames a file, by renaming it and checking 
    return value.

E. Timestamps
    File create, last modified and accessed times all stored in file info
    struct. Tested by printing file info, then writing to file, waiting 5
    seconds, then printiing again. Last modified and accessed time updates,
    created does not.

Extra Credit

D. Read-only and writeByte Support
    Implemented tfs_makeRO() and tfs_makeRW() which flip a read-only flag.
    Also updates modified time. Tested in demo file by setting file to READONLY,
    then checks to make sure writeFile, deleteFile, and writeByte all return 
    READ_ONLY error. tfs_writeByte() writes 1 byte at the current file pointer 
    position. If position is the end of file, file is reallocated cleanly using 
    tfs_writeFile. Tested in demo by overwriting both bytes 0 and EOF, then 
    checking to make sure they were written to correctly. 

H. File System Consistency Checks
    New function tfs_checkConsistency(). On every tfs_mount() call, function
    parses all free and inode blocks in the system. Confirms all block types
    are valid, every block is referenced exactly once, and each block has the 
    correct magic byte.
